## rc-calc2 | Calc 2 and 3 concept check | maths | 2 | calc2 | rc-calc
quiz 3 | Integration by parts comes from which differentiation rule?
* The product rule
o The chain rule
o The quotient rule
o The power rule
= ∫u dv = uv − ∫v du.
> Next

quiz 3 | u-substitution reverses which rule?
* The chain rule
o The product rule
o The quotient rule
o L'Hôpital's rule
= Spot an inner function and its derivative.
> Next

quiz 3 | An improper integral is one where…
* A limit of integration is infinite, or the integrand blows up
o The answer is negative
o The function is discontinuous somewhere harmless
o It can't be computed
= Defined as a limit.
> Next

quiz 3 | The ratio test is inconclusive when the limit equals…
* 1
o 0
o ∞
o 1/2
= Try another test.
> Next

quiz 3 | A Taylor series about 0 is also called a…
* Maclaurin series
o Fourier series
o Laurent series
o Power tower
= After Colin Maclaurin.
> Next

quiz 4 | A Fourier series represents a periodic function as a sum of…
* Sines and cosines
o Polynomials
o Exponentials only
o Step functions
= Frequencies that are integer multiples of the fundamental.
> Next

quiz 4 | A homogeneous linear ODE with constant coefficients is solved by trying…
* y = e^(rx)
o y = x^r
o y = ln x
o y = sin x only
= Leading to the characteristic equation.
> Next

quiz 4 | What does a partial derivative ∂f/∂x hold constant?
* All the other variables
o x
o The function's value
o Nothing
= It's the slope along the x-direction.
> Next

quiz 4 | A saddle point of f(x, y) has Hessian determinant…
* Negative
o Positive
o Zero
o Equal to 1
= D = f_xx f_yy − f_xy² < 0.
> Next

quiz 4 | Stokes' theorem relates…
* Circulation around a curve to the curl through a surface it bounds
o Flux through a closed surface to divergence
o Area to perimeter
o Volume to surface area
= ∮F·dr = ∬(∇×F)·dS.
> Next

quiz 4 | The divergence of a curl is always…
* Zero
o One
o The Laplacian
o Undefined
= ∇·(∇×F) = 0.
> Next

quiz 5 | What does the generalised Stokes theorem say?
* ∫ over ∂M of ω = ∫ over M of dω
o All integrals are zero
o d(dω) = ω
o Every form is exact
= It contains the FTC, Green, Stokes and divergence theorems.
> Concept checks complete

## qf-mix | Quick-fire: anything goes | history | 0 | - | qf-hist4
quiz 3 | Which inventor is associated with the light bulb's commercial success?
* Thomas Edison
o Nikola Tesla
o Joseph Swan only
o Alexander Graham Bell
= Joseph Swan developed a working bulb in Britain around the same time; they later merged companies.
> Next

quiz 3 | Who developed the theory of evolution by natural selection alongside Darwin?
* Alfred Russel Wallace
o Gregor Mendel
o Jean-Baptiste Lamarck
o Thomas Huxley
= Their papers were read together in 1858.
> Next

quiz 3 | Which scientist formulated the laws of motion and universal gravitation?
* Isaac Newton
o Galileo
o Kepler
o Einstein
= Principia, 1687.
> Next

quiz 4 | Which ship carried Darwin on his voyage?
* HMS Beagle
o HMS Endeavour
o HMS Victory
o The Mayflower
= 1831–1836, including the Galápagos.
> Next

quiz 4 | Who discovered penicillin?
* Alexander Fleming
o Louis Pasteur
o Joseph Lister
o Edward Jenner
= 1928.
> Next

quiz 4 | Who proposed the heliocentric model in 1543?
* Nicolaus Copernicus
o Galileo
o Ptolemy
o Kepler
= Aristarchus had suggested it around 270 BC.
> Next

quiz 4 | Which scientist is known for the uncertainty principle?
* Werner Heisenberg
o Erwin Schrödinger
o Max Planck
o Paul Dirac
= 1927.
> Next

quiz 4 | Who first proposed that continents drift?
* Alfred Wegener
o Charles Lyell
o James Hutton
o Arthur Holmes
= 1912. Holmes later suggested mantle convection as the mechanism.
> Next

quiz 4 | Which mathematician is known for the incompleteness theorems?
* Kurt Gödel
o David Hilbert
o Alan Turing
o Bertrand Russell
= 1931.
> Next

quiz 4 | Who wrote A Brief History of Time?
* Stephen Hawking
o Carl Sagan
o Richard Feynman
o Brian Greene
= 1988. It spent years on bestseller lists.
> Next

quiz 4 | Who discovered the structure of benzene (a ring)?
* August Kekulé
o Dmitri Mendeleev
o Linus Pauling
o John Dalton
= 1865.
> Next

quiz 4 | Who proposed atomic theory in its modern chemical form (1808)?
* John Dalton
o Democritus
o Niels Bohr
o Ernest Rutherford
= Elements made of identical atoms combining in simple ratios.
> Next

quiz 5 | Who first proved that the square root of 2 is irrational (traditionally)?
* The Pythagoreans
o Euclid
o Archimedes
o Euler
= Legend says Hippasus was drowned for revealing it. Probably a myth.
> Anything-goes round complete

## qf-final2 | Quick-fire: last round | physics | 0 | - | qf-phys2
quiz 3 | What's the unit of electrical resistance?
* Ohm
o Volt
o Amp
o Watt
= Ω.
> Next

quiz 3 | Which particle is responsible for electric current in metals?
* Electrons
o Protons
o Neutrons
o Photons
= Conduction electrons drift slowly; the signal travels fast.
> Next

quiz 3 | What is the speed of sound in air at room temperature?
* About 343 m/s
o About 3 × 10⁸ m/s
o About 34 m/s
o About 1,500 m/s
= 1,500 m/s is roughly sound in water.
> Next

quiz 4 | What happens to the period of a pendulum on the Moon?
* It gets longer
o It gets shorter
o It stays the same
o It stops swinging
= T = 2π√(L/g), and g is about a sixth of Earth's.
> Next

quiz 4 | Which phase change absorbs the most energy per kilogram for water?
* Boiling (liquid to gas)
o Melting
o Freezing
o Condensing
= About 2,260 kJ/kg versus 334 kJ/kg for melting.
> Next

quiz 4 | Doubling the distance from a point light source makes it…
* A quarter as bright
o Half as bright
o The same
o Twice as bright
= Inverse square law.
> Next

quiz 4 | What's the main reason the sky is dark at night?
* The universe has a finite age, so light from far stars hasn't reached us
o Stars are too few
o Dust blocks all starlight
o The Sun is behind Earth (only)
= Olbers' paradox: an infinite, eternal, static universe would have a bright sky everywhere.
> Next

quiz 5 | Which equation describes how a quantum state evolves in time?
* The Schrödinger equation
o Maxwell's equations
o Newton's second law
o The Navier–Stokes equations
= iħ∂ψ/∂t = Hψ.
> Last round complete
